\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Form-preserving maps and the half-turn Clifford automorphism}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring, #{M_1,M_2} be #{k}-modules
with quadratic forms #{q_1,q_2}, and let #{f:M_1\to M_2} be
linear with
##{q_2(f(v))=q_1(v)\qquad(v\in M_1).}
This form-preserving map induces a unique unital #{k}-algebra map
##{\operatorname{Cl}(f):\operatorname{Cl}(q_1)\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Cl}(q_2)}
satisfying #{\operatorname{Cl}(f)(\iota_1(v))=\iota_2(f(v))}.
This is the underlying Clifford map in
[isometry transport](fcap-0029). It requires no inverse for #{f}.
The defining square is:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  map/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (m1) at (0,0) {$M_1$};
\node (m2) at (4,0) {$M_2$};
\node (c1) at (0,-2) {$\operatorname{Cl}(q_1)$};
\node (c2) at (4,-2) {$\operatorname{Cl}(q_2)$};
\draw[map] (m1) -- node[above=2mm] {$f$} (m2);
\draw[map] (c1) -- node[below=2mm] {$\operatorname{Cl}(f)$} (c2);
\draw[map] (m1) -- node[left=2mm] {$\iota_1$} (c1);
\draw[map] (m2) -- node[right=2mm] {$\iota_2$} (c2);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The top and vertical arrows are linear maps; the bottom arrow
is a unital algebra map. The square commutes as a square of
linear maps. For composable form-preserving maps #{f,g},
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cl}(\operatorname{id})&=\operatorname{id},\\
\operatorname{Cl}(g\circ f)&=\operatorname{Cl}(g)\circ\operatorname{Cl}(f).
\end{aligned}}
If #{f} is a linear isomorphism, #{\operatorname{Cl}(f)} is an
algebra isomorphism with inverse #{\operatorname{Cl}(f^{-1})}.}

\proof{
\p{The linear map #{v\mapsto\iota_2(f(v))} has square
#{q_2(f(v))1=q_1(v)1}. Apply the
[universal property](fdip-000P) to obtain the unique extension.
For the identity, the induced map and the identity algebra map
agree on every generator, hence are equal by uniqueness.
For composition, both proposed maps send #{\iota_1(v)} to
#{\iota_3(g(f(v)))}; uniqueness again proves equality.
If #{f} is invertible, set #{v=f^{-1}(w)} in form preservation
to get #{q_1(f^{-1}(w))=q_2(w)}. Thus the inverse is also
form-preserving. The two composition identities give mutually
inverse algebra maps.}
}

\p{Meinrenken derives this functoriality for bilinear-form-preserving
maps in \citet{Section 2.2.2, p. 29}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
Over his characteristic-zero field, polarization equates that
condition with preservation of #{q}. The proof here applies
directly to quadratic forms over commutative rings. Mathlib's
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.map},
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.map_apply_ι},
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.map_id},
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.map_comp_map} and
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.equivOfIsometry}
provide the extension, generator equation, identity, composition
and inverse construction. Their input is a linear map preserving
the quadratic form, or a linear equivalence for the last result.}

\p{Return to #{q=q_{t,m}} on #{V=\mathbb R^4}. The same
[half-turn](fdip-000J) sends #{\delta_i} to #{\delta_{i+2}},
with indices modulo four. It satisfies #{P^2=I} and
#{P^TQP=Q}, so #{q(Pv)=q(v)}. Therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
\Theta&=\operatorname{Cl}(P),\\
\Theta(c_i)&=c_{i+2},\qquad \Theta^2=\operatorname{id}.
\end{aligned}}
The matrix equation follows by shifting both indices of #{Q}
by two: its diagonal, its paired off-diagonal entries #{-1} and
#{-t}, and its zeros are preserved. Composition gives
#{\Theta^2=\operatorname{Cl}(P^2)=\operatorname{id}}.
Moreover #{\delta_0\ne\delta_2}, so
[injectivity of #{\iota_q}](fdip-000P) gives #{c_0\ne c_2}.
Thus #{\Theta} is nontrivial and has order exactly two.}

\p{The [real half-turn sectors](fdip-000L)
#{V_\pm=\ker(P\mp I)} are orthogonal for #{b_q}. Indeed,
#{P^TQP=Q} gives #{b_q(Pu,Pv)=b_q(u,v)}. For
#{u\in V_+} and #{v\in V_-}, the left side is
#{b_q(u,-v)=-b_q(u,v)}. Over #{\mathbb R} this forces
#{b_q(u,v)=0}, and hence
##{\iota_q(u)\iota_q(v)=-\iota_q(v)\iota_q(u).}
This is the generic orthogonality consequence
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_comm_of_isOrtho}.
The cross-sector vector generators anticommute; the direct sum
of sectors does not make them commuting factors of an ordinary
tensor-product algebra.}

\p{Preservation of the form is essential. Compare #{q_{1,1}}
and #{q_{2,1}} while keeping the coordinate labels unchanged.
At #{\delta_0},
##{q_{1,1}(\delta_0)=3,\qquad q_{2,1}(\delta_0)=4.}
Thus the identity linear map is not form-preserving. Write
#{c_i^{(1)},c_i^{(2)}} for the two sets of generators.
A unital real algebra map with
#{c_i^{(1)}\mapsto c_i^{(2)}} would send
#{(c_0^{(1)})^2=3\,1} to #{(c_0^{(2)})^2=3\,1},
but the target square is #{4\,1}. Its
[injective scalar map](fdip-000P) rules this out.
This excludes precisely the unchanged-label assignment. It does
not exclude other isometries between the positive forms or an
abstract isomorphism of their Clifford algebras.}

\p{The construction transports a supplied configuration form and
its discrete symmetry. It supplies no spacetime derivative,
Dirac operator or Lorentzian interpretation. Those structures
need additional data and compatibility conditions.}
